class string matches neither, which is exactly what the captured errors showed (`does not match the regex pattern`, `string found, object expected`).\n\n**Fix:** Stripped the `$$Lambda/0x<address>` suffix down to the enclosing class name, matching the convention already used elsewhere in the same file (e.g. line 328/348/5151 already reference `MethodUtils`/`RandomUtils` without a lambda suffix). This made the entries schema-valid.\n\nThat surfaced a second-order issue: since the JVM assigns each lambda a different random address per run, the 4 `MethodUtils` entries and 5 `RandomUtils` entries collapsed into exact duplicates of each other once normalized. I removed the redundant copies, keeping one of each.\n\n`./gradlew checkMetadataFiles -Pcoordinates=org.apache.pulsar:pulsar-client-all:4.2.5` now passes.\n\n★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\nJDK lambdas are compiled to *hidden classes* at runtime with synthetic names like `Outer$$Lambda/0x<heap-address>` — the hex suffix is not stable across JVM runs (it changes with class-loading order/ASLR-like allocation). A native-image tracing agent that naively records \"what class was reached\" for reflective access performed inside a lambda will capture this throwaway name verbatim. GraalVM's metadata schema anticipated this by giving lambdas their own structured descriptor (`declaringClass` + `interfaces`) instead of treating them as nameable types — but populating that correctly requires knowing the functional interface the lambda implements, which a condition-tracking agent doesn't always retain. Falling back to the enclosing class is the standard, information-preserving degradation: the reflection access conceptually becomes reachable once the enclosing class is reached, lambda or not.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────","ttft_ms":3084,"type":"result","duration_ms":194006,"uuid":"cac917a4-f647-4a24-b7ef-409165f6a85b","ttft_stream_ms":1910,"time_to_request_ms":208,"first_content_frame_ms":1